1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,021
Tonight, more than a thousand people will squeeze

2
00:00:03,031 --> 00:00:05,671
into a Boston nightclub licensed for about four

3
00:00:05,681 --> 00:00:06,606
hundred and sixty.

4
00:00:06,843 --> 00:00:07,743
And you are one of them.

5
00:00:08,242 --> 00:00:09,808
Before you walk in, look at the front

6
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:10,343
door.

7
00:00:10,593 --> 00:00:11,731
It's a revolving door.

8
00:00:11,993 --> 00:00:12,906
It spins.

9
00:00:13,156 --> 00:00:13,868
Remember it.

10
00:00:14,370 --> 00:00:16,087
In a few hours, that door will help

11
00:00:16,097 --> 00:00:18,397
decide who lives, and who doesn't.

12
00:00:18,891 --> 00:00:21,477
In about fifteen minutes, four hundred and ninety-two

13
00:00:21,487 --> 00:00:22,549
people will be gone.

14
00:00:23,059 --> 00:00:25,440
Hour by hour, you'll face the choices they

15
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:26,163
faced.

16
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:28,440
And by the end, you'll know whether you

17
00:00:28,450 --> 00:00:29,475
would have survived.

18
00:00:32,623 --> 00:00:36,154
It's Saturday, November twenty-eighth, 1942.

19
00:00:36,379 --> 00:00:38,281
America has been at war for less than

20
00:00:38,291 --> 00:00:38,879
a year.

21
00:00:39,369 --> 00:00:42,520
You're twenty-one, a Navy sailor home on leave

22
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:43,942
for Thanksgiving weekend.

23
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:46,307
Your girl, Ruth, has come into Boston to

24
00:00:46,317 --> 00:00:46,930
see you.

25
00:00:47,442 --> 00:00:49,691
This afternoon you're at Fenway Park, for the

26
00:00:49,701 --> 00:00:51,826
biggest college football game of the season.

27
00:00:52,088 --> 00:00:54,501
Boston College against Holy Cross.

28
00:00:54,992 --> 00:00:57,576
Boston College hasn't lost all year.

29
00:00:57,826 --> 00:00:59,841
It's ranked number one, and a big bowl

30
00:00:59,851 --> 00:01:01,826
game is all but promised.

31
00:01:02,317 --> 00:01:03,994
Then the impossible happens.

32
00:01:04,256 --> 00:01:06,031
Little Holy Cross wins.

33
00:01:06,369 --> 00:01:08,031
Fifty-five to twelve.

34
00:01:08,532 --> 00:01:10,871
Boston College had planned a victory party for

35
00:01:10,881 --> 00:01:14,094
tonight, at the city's most glamorous nightclub,

36
00:01:14,181 --> 00:01:15,709
the Cocoanut Grove.

37
00:01:15,719 --> 00:01:17,169
Now the party is cancelled.

38
00:01:17,676 --> 00:01:19,418
For the team, it's the worst day of

39
00:01:19,428 --> 00:01:20,078
the season.

40
00:01:20,315 --> 00:01:22,943
Later, people will say that defeat saved their

41
00:01:22,953 --> 00:01:23,715
lives.

42
00:01:24,187 --> 00:01:25,670
But the club won't be empty.

43
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:28,960
It's a holiday weekend, in wartime, and the

44
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:31,423
city is full of servicemen with a little

45
00:01:31,433 --> 00:01:32,433
money to spend.

46
00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:35,276
By seven o'clock, you and Ruth are standing

47
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:37,789
on Piedmont Street, in the cold, under a

48
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:39,024
pink neon sign.

49
00:01:39,525 --> 00:01:42,629
The Cocoanut Grove opened in the 1920s.

50
00:01:42,904 --> 00:01:45,041
For a while it belonged to a gangster

51
00:01:45,091 --> 00:01:48,894
named Charles Solomon, until he was killed in

52
00:01:48,904 --> 00:01:49,304
1933.

53
00:01:49,562 --> 00:01:52,172
Now it's run by his lawyer, Barney Welansky,

54
00:01:52,385 --> 00:01:54,160
a man with friends at City Hall.

55
00:01:54,647 --> 00:01:55,932
He's said to have bragged that he didn't

56
00:01:55,942 --> 00:01:57,717
need to follow the building code.

57
00:01:58,218 --> 00:02:00,923
And just days before tonight, the club passed

58
00:02:00,933 --> 00:02:03,108
a fire inspection, with a good rating.

59
00:02:03,552 --> 00:02:05,131
You step into the revolving door.

60
00:02:05,381 --> 00:02:06,993
It turns, and you're inside.

61
00:02:07,293 --> 00:02:09,046
For most of the people here, it's the

62
00:02:09,056 --> 00:02:10,431
only way out they know.

63
00:02:10,923 --> 00:02:12,076
It's warm and loud.

64
00:02:12,301 --> 00:02:14,439
A girl at the checkroom takes your coats,

65
00:02:14,526 --> 00:02:15,814
and you head into the main room.

66
00:02:16,312 --> 00:02:18,569
Walking in is like stepping into the tropics.

67
00:02:18,857 --> 00:02:19,944
Fake palm trees.

68
00:02:20,182 --> 00:02:21,032
Leather walls.

69
00:02:21,282 --> 00:02:23,432
A ceiling covered in billowing cloth.

70
00:02:23,935 --> 00:02:26,948
In summer, the roof even rolls back, so

71
00:02:26,958 --> 00:02:28,495
you can dance under the stars.

72
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:30,062
A band is playing.

73
00:02:30,312 --> 00:02:32,390
Waiters weave between the tables with trays

74
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:33,175
of drinks.

75
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:35,952
For a few hours, nobody is thinking about

76
00:02:35,962 --> 00:02:36,637
the war.

77
00:02:37,102 --> 00:02:39,260
Tonight, every table is full.

78
00:02:39,498 --> 00:02:41,888
People are squeezed in at the bars, on

79
00:02:41,898 --> 00:02:43,710
the dance floor, in the doorways.

80
00:02:44,192 --> 00:02:45,317
Nobody is counting.

81
00:02:45,592 --> 00:02:48,169
But investigators will later estimate more than

82
00:02:48,179 --> 00:02:49,804
a thousand people inside.

83
00:02:50,067 --> 00:02:52,632
The legal limit, by most accounts, was about

84
00:02:52,642 --> 00:02:54,004
four hundred and sixty.

85
00:02:54,459 --> 00:02:56,522
There are other doors, but you'd never know

86
00:02:56,532 --> 00:02:56,832
it.

87
00:02:57,082 --> 00:02:58,645
None of them has an exit sign.

88
00:02:59,151 --> 00:03:01,381
A door onto Shawmut Street is locked.

89
00:03:01,631 --> 00:03:03,768
Another has a bolt that keeps it shut.

90
00:03:04,043 --> 00:03:06,383
Some doors are hidden, and only the staff

91
00:03:06,393 --> 00:03:07,343
know where they are.

92
00:03:07,809 --> 00:03:09,791
One old exit has even been replaced by

93
00:03:09,801 --> 00:03:10,726
a brick wall.

94
00:03:11,226 --> 00:03:13,532
People will say the owner kept doors locked

95
00:03:13,594 --> 00:03:16,007
so nobody could slip out without paying.

96
00:03:16,269 --> 00:03:17,394
That was never proven.

97
00:03:17,896 --> 00:03:20,950
Across the room, you spot a famous face.

98
00:03:21,213 --> 00:03:24,015
Buck Jones, the cowboy movie star, is in

99
00:03:24,025 --> 00:03:26,325
town on a tour selling war bonds.

100
00:03:26,804 --> 00:03:28,345
He's been feeling sick all day.

101
00:03:28,595 --> 00:03:30,273
But friends talked him into coming out for

102
00:03:30,283 --> 00:03:30,820
dinner.

103
00:03:31,301 --> 00:03:32,643
Ruth tugs your sleeve.

104
00:03:32,905 --> 00:03:35,505
Downstairs there's a cozy bar with a piano,

105
00:03:35,805 --> 00:03:37,055
the Melody Lounge.

106
00:03:37,305 --> 00:03:39,518
Do you go down, or stay up here,

107
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:40,393
near the front door?

108
00:03:40,890 --> 00:03:41,603
You go down.

109
00:03:41,866 --> 00:03:43,678
So do a lot of young couples tonight.

110
00:03:44,127 --> 00:03:46,427
The stairs are narrow, only about four feet

111
00:03:46,437 --> 00:03:46,974
wide.

112
00:03:47,249 --> 00:03:49,402
They're the only public way in or out

113
00:03:49,412 --> 00:03:50,062
of this room.

114
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:52,398
It's dark down here, on purpose.

115
00:03:52,661 --> 00:03:55,313
The ceiling is draped in blue satin, with

116
00:03:55,323 --> 00:03:57,186
tiny lights like stars.

117
00:03:57,675 --> 00:03:59,939
A pianist is playing, and people are singing

118
00:03:59,949 --> 00:04:00,499
along.

119
00:04:00,761 --> 00:04:02,276
It's the kind of place where you lose

120
00:04:02,286 --> 00:04:03,236
track of time.

121
00:04:03,738 --> 00:04:06,261
In the corner stands an artificial palm tree,

122
00:04:06,411 --> 00:04:08,848
with a light bulb hidden among its leaves.

123
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:10,858
A young soldier at the table next to

124
00:04:10,868 --> 00:04:13,731
it reaches up and unscrews that bulb.

125
00:04:13,993 --> 00:04:15,808
He wants a little darkness, to kiss his

126
00:04:15,818 --> 00:04:16,356
date.

127
00:04:16,857 --> 00:04:19,753
The bartender notices, and sends a busboy over

128
00:04:19,763 --> 00:04:20,663
to put it back.

129
00:04:21,141 --> 00:04:23,117
The busboy is Stanley Tomaszewski.

130
00:04:23,392 --> 00:04:26,332
He's sixteen, a high-school kid working long

131
00:04:26,342 --> 00:04:28,492
shifts for a couple of dollars and tips.

132
00:04:28,978 --> 00:04:31,005
In the dark, he can't find the socket.

133
00:04:31,255 --> 00:04:32,417
So he lights a match.

134
00:04:32,931 --> 00:04:35,025
He finds it, screws the bulb back in,

135
00:04:35,163 --> 00:04:36,550
and blows out the match.

136
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:39,138
At least, that's what he'll swear later.

137
00:04:39,624 --> 00:04:41,043
It's about ten fifteen.

138
00:04:41,293 --> 00:04:44,130
Upstairs, the big floor show is running late.

139
00:04:44,627 --> 00:04:46,738
A moment later, someone points at the palm

140
00:04:46,748 --> 00:04:47,298
tree.

141
00:04:47,548 --> 00:04:49,173
There's a flicker in the leaves.

142
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:51,801
Then the fronds burst into flames.

143
00:04:52,283 --> 00:04:55,100
Bartenders throw water and seltzer at it.

144
00:04:55,388 --> 00:04:57,003
For a few seconds, it looks like they

145
00:04:57,013 --> 00:04:58,050
might put it out.

146
00:04:58,544 --> 00:05:00,358
A sailor who was there will remember it

147
00:05:00,368 --> 00:05:01,505
started slowly.

148
00:05:01,768 --> 00:05:04,755
Then, suddenly, it raced across the ceiling.

149
00:05:05,253 --> 00:05:06,620
Burning cloth falls.

150
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:08,358
Thick smoke fills the room.

151
00:05:08,608 --> 00:05:09,633
Then the lights go out.

152
00:05:10,135 --> 00:05:12,228
Now you face the hardest choice of your

153
00:05:12,238 --> 00:05:12,826
life.

154
00:05:13,063 --> 00:05:15,953
Everyone around you is pushing toward the narrow

155
00:05:15,963 --> 00:05:17,951
stairs, the way they came in.

156
00:05:18,449 --> 00:05:20,520
But a waiter behind the bar is waving

157
00:05:20,530 --> 00:05:23,232
people toward a door into the dark service

158
00:05:23,242 --> 00:05:24,055
corridors.

159
00:05:24,305 --> 00:05:26,292
Do you follow the crowd, or the waiter?

160
00:05:26,790 --> 00:05:28,693
The crowd heads for the only exit it

161
00:05:28,703 --> 00:05:29,391
knows.

162
00:05:29,641 --> 00:05:31,153
The staff know other ways out.

163
00:05:31,652 --> 00:05:34,912
Upstairs, the fire bursts out of the stairway

164
00:05:34,924 --> 00:05:36,362
and into the entrance hall.

165
00:05:36,836 --> 00:05:39,005
Within minutes, it has spread through the whole

166
00:05:39,015 --> 00:05:41,892
club, fed by the cloth ceiling, the palm

167
00:05:41,902 --> 00:05:43,665
trees and the furniture.

168
00:05:44,126 --> 00:05:46,331
Hundreds of people rush for the one door

169
00:05:46,341 --> 00:05:47,028
they know.

170
00:05:47,291 --> 00:05:48,303
The revolving door.

171
00:05:48,802 --> 00:05:49,760
A few get out.

172
00:05:50,022 --> 00:05:51,925
Then so many people push from both sides

173
00:05:51,935 --> 00:05:52,985
that it jams.

174
00:05:53,463 --> 00:05:55,185
A door built to let people in a

175
00:05:55,195 --> 00:05:57,420
few at a time has become a wall.

176
00:05:57,907 --> 00:06:01,044
Nearby, a brand-new lounge has its own exit.

177
00:06:01,307 --> 00:06:04,184
But that door opens inward, and the crowd

178
00:06:04,194 --> 00:06:06,169
pushes it shut with its own weight.

179
00:06:06,656 --> 00:06:09,554
In the dining room, a few people smash

180
00:06:09,564 --> 00:06:10,389
windows.

181
00:06:10,627 --> 00:06:13,067
Others are pulled to safety by waiters who

182
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:14,452
know the hidden doors.

183
00:06:14,938 --> 00:06:16,455
One officer will later say that when the

184
00:06:16,465 --> 00:06:19,267
lights went out, everyone's good sense seemed

185
00:06:19,277 --> 00:06:20,190
to go out with them.

186
00:06:20,689 --> 00:06:22,243
But nobody here was foolish.

187
00:06:22,493 --> 00:06:24,658
In the dark and the smoke, nobody could

188
00:06:24,668 --> 00:06:25,693
see a way out.

189
00:06:26,142 --> 00:06:28,660
At his table upstairs, Buck Jones is one

190
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:30,483
of the most famous men in the room.

191
00:06:30,988 --> 00:06:32,300
He has starred in more than a hundred

192
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:33,110
westerns.

193
00:06:33,347 --> 00:06:35,685
On screen, he always finds a way out.

194
00:06:36,179 --> 00:06:37,582
Tonight there's no script.

195
00:06:37,832 --> 00:06:40,747
He's carried out alive, and rushed to Massachusetts

196
00:06:40,757 --> 00:06:41,757
General Hospital.

197
00:06:42,253 --> 00:06:44,027
Two days later, he dies.

198
00:06:44,290 --> 00:06:46,002
The news shocks the whole country.

199
00:06:46,510 --> 00:06:49,482
Outside, by strange luck, fire engines are already

200
00:06:49,492 --> 00:06:50,367
close by.

201
00:06:50,617 --> 00:06:52,520
A few streets away, they were putting out

202
00:06:52,530 --> 00:06:53,492
a burning car.

203
00:06:53,966 --> 00:06:56,476
A firefighter sees smoke pouring out of the

204
00:06:56,486 --> 00:06:57,136
Grove.

205
00:06:57,399 --> 00:06:59,961
More alarms go out, one after another.

206
00:07:00,429 --> 00:07:03,215
Sailors, soldiers and Coast Guardsmen in the

207
00:07:03,225 --> 00:07:04,700
street rush in to help.

208
00:07:04,963 --> 00:07:07,415
They form lines, and break through walls and

209
00:07:07,425 --> 00:07:08,113
doors.

210
00:07:08,606 --> 00:07:11,147
The Navy sends trucks and medical teams from

211
00:07:11,157 --> 00:07:12,957
its shipyard across the harbor.

212
00:07:13,442 --> 00:07:15,127
A crowd gathers in the cold.

213
00:07:15,377 --> 00:07:17,204
Some are searching for friends and family who

214
00:07:17,214 --> 00:07:19,252
went inside and haven't come out.

215
00:07:19,752 --> 00:07:22,139
Firefighters fight their way inside.

216
00:07:22,389 --> 00:07:24,117
What they find near the doors, they will

217
00:07:24,127 --> 00:07:25,139
never forget.

218
00:07:25,633 --> 00:07:27,667
But down in the kitchen, they find people

219
00:07:27,677 --> 00:07:28,677
still alive.

220
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:31,627
Some of them hid inside a walk-in refrigerator,

221
00:07:31,827 --> 00:07:33,452
where the smoke couldn't reach them.

222
00:07:33,951 --> 00:07:35,829
From the first flicker in that palm tree,

223
00:07:36,067 --> 00:07:38,479
the worst was over in about fifteen minutes.

224
00:07:38,955 --> 00:07:41,449
At Boston City Hospital, the victims arrive

225
00:07:41,459 --> 00:07:43,809
about one every eleven seconds.

226
00:07:44,146 --> 00:07:45,634
Three hundred in a single hour.

227
00:07:46,131 --> 00:07:48,787
Across town, Massachusetts General takes in

228
00:07:48,797 --> 00:07:50,950
more than a hundred people in about two

229
00:07:50,960 --> 00:07:51,660
hours.

230
00:07:52,141 --> 00:07:55,072
Doctors, nurses and volunteers work through

231
00:07:55,082 --> 00:07:55,732
the night.

232
00:07:55,982 --> 00:07:58,382
One hospital had practiced a disaster drill

233
00:07:58,507 --> 00:07:59,720
just the week before.

234
00:08:00,214 --> 00:08:02,250
Many are hurt less by the flames than

235
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:03,797
by the smoke they breathed.

236
00:08:04,294 --> 00:08:06,671
Doctors there try a gentler way to treat

237
00:08:06,681 --> 00:08:09,721
burns, with soft dressings instead of harsh

238
00:08:09,731 --> 00:08:10,456
dyes.

239
00:08:10,718 --> 00:08:12,983
It works, and it changes burn care for

240
00:08:12,993 --> 00:08:13,406
good.

241
00:08:13,909 --> 00:08:16,398
Some patients also get a new drug almost

242
00:08:16,408 --> 00:08:18,496
no civilian has ever received.

243
00:08:18,746 --> 00:08:19,496
Penicillin.

244
00:08:20,007 --> 00:08:22,851
And a psychiatrist named Erich Lindemann talks

245
00:08:22,861 --> 00:08:25,299
with survivors and grieving families.

246
00:08:25,549 --> 00:08:28,014
His work becomes one of the first scientific

247
00:08:28,024 --> 00:08:29,136
studies of grief.

248
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:30,753
So what started the fire?

249
00:08:31,015 --> 00:08:34,478
Almost at once, the newspapers blamed the busboy.

250
00:08:34,976 --> 00:08:37,258
But the fire commissioner's official report

251
00:08:37,268 --> 00:08:38,180
couldn't prove it.

252
00:08:38,430 --> 00:08:39,993
It called the cause unknown.

253
00:08:40,504 --> 00:08:42,397
Historians still argue about it.

254
00:08:42,660 --> 00:08:45,675
Some think leaking refrigerator gas, or faulty

255
00:08:45,685 --> 00:08:48,897
wiring, helped the fire spread so fast.

256
00:08:49,391 --> 00:08:51,352
Stanley Tomaszewski was cleared.

257
00:08:51,602 --> 00:08:53,117
But he carried the blame for the rest

258
00:08:53,127 --> 00:08:53,952
of his life.

259
00:08:54,202 --> 00:08:56,265
He died in 1994.

260
00:08:56,513 --> 00:08:59,567
Barney Welansky was convicted of manslaughter,

261
00:08:59,867 --> 00:09:02,132
and sentenced to twelve to fifteen years in

262
00:09:02,142 --> 00:09:02,680
prison.

263
00:09:03,179 --> 00:09:04,622
He served less than four.

264
00:09:04,897 --> 00:09:08,900
He was released, very ill, in 1946, and

265
00:09:08,910 --> 00:09:10,472
died only weeks later.

266
00:09:10,954 --> 00:09:12,236
The fire changed the law.

267
00:09:12,486 --> 00:09:14,511
Exits must be marked and lit.

268
00:09:14,761 --> 00:09:16,736
Exit doors must swing outward.

269
00:09:16,999 --> 00:09:19,586
Decorations can't be easily flammable.

270
00:09:20,048 --> 00:09:23,354
And a revolving door now needs ordinary swinging

271
00:09:23,364 --> 00:09:24,677
doors right beside it.

272
00:09:25,168 --> 00:09:27,350
The club itself was torn down years ago.

273
00:09:27,612 --> 00:09:30,040
Today, a small plaque in the sidewalk marks

274
00:09:30,050 --> 00:09:31,250
the place where it stood.

275
00:09:31,732 --> 00:09:32,885
So, would you have survived?

276
00:09:33,122 --> 00:09:35,135
Of the more than a thousand people inside,

277
00:09:35,185 --> 00:09:36,760
four hundred and ninety-two died.

278
00:09:37,259 --> 00:09:39,813
Many survivors did one of three things.

279
00:09:40,075 --> 00:09:41,938
They moved at the very first smoke.

280
00:09:42,188 --> 00:09:43,113
They found a window.

281
00:09:43,363 --> 00:09:45,440
Or they followed someone who knew another way

282
00:09:45,450 --> 00:09:45,900
out.

283
00:09:46,343 --> 00:09:48,445
The waiters and kitchen workers knew the back

284
00:09:48,455 --> 00:09:49,305
corridors.

285
00:09:49,542 --> 00:09:51,567
Many of the people who followed them lived.

286
00:09:52,066 --> 00:09:54,470
The people who waited, to finish a drink,

287
00:09:54,507 --> 00:09:56,172
to find a coat, to look for a

288
00:09:56,182 --> 00:09:58,920
friend, had far less time than they thought.

289
00:09:59,406 --> 00:10:01,382
If you followed that waiter into the dark,

290
00:10:01,495 --> 00:10:02,857
you probably made it out.

291
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:04,360
If you went with the crowd to the

292
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:07,172
stairs and the spinning door, your chances were

293
00:10:07,182 --> 00:10:08,145
far worse.

294
00:10:08,612 --> 00:10:10,156
Remember that revolving door?

295
00:10:10,394 --> 00:10:12,496
Next time you walk through one, look beside

296
00:10:12,506 --> 00:10:12,806
it.

297
00:10:13,069 --> 00:10:15,196
Those ordinary doors are there because of the

298
00:10:15,206 --> 00:10:16,619
Cocoanut Grove.

299
00:10:16,853 --> 00:10:18,167
Which choice would you have made?

300
00:10:18,430 --> 00:10:19,580
The crowd, or the waiter?

301
00:10:19,842 --> 00:10:20,992
Tell me in the comments.

